  "title": "The arras contract decides what you bought",
  "source_article": "property-contracts.txt, buying-10-steps.txt, bank-guarantees.txt",
  "post_text": "The arras draft usually lands in your inbox two days before signing day. That window isn't a courtesy. It's your only real chance to change the contract.\n\nBuying from another country makes those two days matter even more, because that's the slot you and your legal representative get to read it properly. Most buyers check the price, skim the rest and sign. Which is a shame, because the arras decides what you actually bought.\n\nBy then you've probably already signed an offer contract with the agency and transferred around 2,000 euros to secure the offer. If the seller says no, that comes back. If they say yes, you can roll it into the agency fees, which run 3 to 5 percent. Ask early who pays those, seller or buyer, because it varies by agency and by property.\n\nThen the arras. The agency normally adapts a standard contract, filling in the property details as they appear on the Nota Simple. Fine so far. But beyond price, the description has to be accurate, address, size and layout. It should pin down the completion date, usually two to three months after signing so your documents and mortgage have time. It should spell out the penalties, and they cut both ways: pull out and you lose your 10 percent deposit, but if the seller pulls out, they pay you double. And it should capture the specifics. Which furniture stays. Which repairs the seller covers. Whether appliances are included, and whether water and electricity will actually be on.\n\nIf it's not written in the arras, it was never agreed. That's the whole game. Once both sides sign, you're bound to complete or pay the penalty.\n\nThe money deserves a thought too. That 10 percent often goes straight to the owners, which is common in Spain, but a safeguarded account at a financial institution is safer. Ask for it.\n\nWith a mortgage, the FEIN comes next, and it has to be signed at least 10 days before the mortgage deed. That wait is the law protecting you, so use it. Book the notary at least 24 hours ahead, and bring a translator if your Spanish isn't up to a legal reading.\n\nBuying a new build? Then you'll also want completion date clauses and the bank guarantee that protects staged payments.\n\nSo read the draft the day it arrives, line by line, and send your amendments before signing day. At the table it's too late.\n\n#SpainProperty #BuyingInSpain #MoveToSpain",
